00:00:18.369 --> 00:00:20.310
Hey guys, welcome to Visual Effects Artist React

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:24.640
This is the show where we take a look at your favorite movie clips, and we tell you why they're good!

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.560
And sometimes we tell you they're bad.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.380
Fun fact, uh, this is kind of like how they shot Lord of the Rings.

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:30.740
This is how they made–
(laugh)

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:35.460
Frodo and Bilbo Baggins look so tiny compared to the big ol' Gandalf.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:38.420
This is— This is special behind-the-scenes footage of Lord of the Rings.

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:39.399
(laugh)

00:00:39.399 --> 00:00:43.139
Guess what? We got some great clips. We got some janky clips. So we're gonna look at them today.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:45.980
Just take a look at a Russian film called T-34.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:47.560
Isn't that a calculator?

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:48.960
(laugh)

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:52.500
*fwoom*

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:53.440
Oooh-

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:56.380
Their muzzle flashes from these tanks are so good.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:59.640
Yeah, these are world-class, like, slow-mo particle sims.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:04.680
(Russian)

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.080
Dude, look at that slow-mo bullet!

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:10.360
What kind of bullet was that?

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:11.980
It's a tank bullet. 
It's a tank bullet. (x2)

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:14.840
Oh, right in the face??

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:16.420
noooOOOOO
W-What—

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:18.420
—he gets shot in the head with a tank round!?

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:18.940
What??

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:24.380
In the frickin' head?

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:26.380
Someone wrote this for Nico.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.500
Because, every time we have a Corridor brainstorming session,

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:31.440
He's always like- I have an idea.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:35.040
We- Let's follow a bullet really far from one place to another, all right?

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.840
And this whole movie is just... that.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:39.440
Well, here's the reason why I like that shot so much.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:41.630
It's 'cause it's actually a relatively easy shot to do.

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:43.380
You just need a way to move a camera smoothly,

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:47.000
whether it's on a Gimbal, or a Steadicam, or it's on a dolly track or something like that

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.480
and then you motion track it,

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.580
and you just put things that are frozen in the air around the motion tracking.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:52.960
So, back in the day,

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:54.469
we did this crazy frozen fight scene,

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:56.250
everything's, like, lingering in the air and,

00:01:56.250 --> 00:02:00.709
All we had to do is have everybody just hold still, and then move the camera through the scene.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:05.960
Then later on we tracked it and added all the debris and the frozen smoke and stuff like that in the air,

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:07.660
And it gets a big reaction out of people.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:08.960
Oh, yeah. Yeah. You're right.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:11.040
Look, I'm just, like, freaking out right now.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:12.240
This is crazy. Yeah.

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:15.320
(intense music)

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:16.980
(yelling)

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:18.780
What?

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:19.760
What? This is how it ends?

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:21.000
Is this the finale?

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.280
Two tanks turning their barrels to each oth–

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:25.460
Of course this is the finale!

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:26.120
You gotta do it manually-

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:27.600
Just trying to go faster.
Oh no!

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:28.680
(panicked heaving)

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:30.420
I'm just saying, this is the coolest thing ever.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:31.260
(laugh)

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:39.220
Oh, and then– It's gonna slide on out.

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:41.040
That big Sabot round.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:42.540
(mimicking slo-mo tank bullet)

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:43.820
Oooh–

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:44.360
Bam.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:45.760
Oh, that multi-milla shockwave,

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:46.320
Sparks!

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:47.240
Sparks are so good!

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:48.480
I love sparks!

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:51.660
Whoa, they even got those flame geysers coming out of the choo-choo hole!

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:54.400
(choo-choo whistle)
(laughing)

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.260
I love the detail of– these shells, like,

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:01.160
liquefying when they strike– objects.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:02.520
It's– It's satisfying.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:05.140
What sets this movie apart from everything else

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:08.040
are the simulations of the fire, the smoke, and the sparks.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:09.240
And the liquid metal.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:12.160
Fire and smoke and liquid are, like,

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:14.940
some of the hardest things you can ask a 
computer to do,

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:15.960
and these guys are like:

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:18.380
"Let's also do it in slow-motion, up close."

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:21.240
So, not only does it have to be high enough detail to look good at regular speed,

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.200
It needs to be high enough detail to look good at slow motion!

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:25.580
This is probably all done in Houdini,

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:28.300
which is a program that has good fluid simulations, and good smoke simulations,

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:30.120
and fire simulations, and spark simulations,

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:31.860
and rigid body, and cloth, and all this stuff

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:34.620
and can do that all under the same roof.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:36.600
Houdini didn't have very good looking fire 
back in the day.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.220
It didn't have very good looking explosions,

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:39.680
But they've been working on it and working on it.

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:43.960
You just get all this crazy, crazy detail from having all the systems interact together.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:47.080
I mean the closest thing we have at the studio for this is, like, X-Particles,

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:50.480
where you can actually have your explosion push an object.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:52.860
It's, like, the only system we have right now that can actually go,

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:56.140
"Oh, well, I can sense there's expanding air in this area,

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:59.260
so I can translate that data to some objects on the ground and have them–

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:02.000
actually kind of move in relationship."

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:04.260
I love how committed this movie is, to just,

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:06.400
the tactics of a tank fight with this crew.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:07.220
It's like–

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:09.280
entire 20-minute sequences that are just a fight.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:11.634
Do you think World of Tanks funded this?

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:12.340
I wonder, man.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:14.720
"Hey everybody, today we're playing World of Tanks."

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:17.880
(Narrator) These people... are our only hope.

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:20.380
What's the name of the mission?

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:21.680
"Guardians."

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:24.100
Now we're watching Russian Avengers.

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:27.480
We've already- reacted to Weta Digital's take on 
The Avengers

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:28.480
So now–

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:30.880
We're taking our budget from 300 million

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:32.983
and we're scaling it down to 5 million.

00:04:32.983 --> 00:04:34.600
And also we're going to Russia.

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:36.580
Oh, man. I cannot wait.

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:40.540
Basically, this is Russia's top superheroes 
fighting baddies.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:44.840
This is the part where they're like,

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:46.220
"All right, Deadpool."

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:48.000
"Or no, Nightcrawler, plus Deadpool."

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:48.960
*whoosh*

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:49.940
(blade slicing)

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:50.860
*gasp*

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:52.120
Oh, sweet anime!

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:52.860
Oh man!

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:53.500
Dude, Russian anime?

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:54.000
Oh!

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:55.380
That was pretty cool.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:56.380
I'm digging this already!

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:57.400
This is pretty sweet!

00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:58.501
As cool as this is,

00:04:58.501 --> 00:05:01.400
it kind of ignores the fact that he would have to–

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:03.850
–get to the same thickness as a sword to be able

00:05:03.850 --> 00:05:05.290
to go through the car.

00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:08.500
(laughing)

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:10.020
He gets reeaally skinny!

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:10.600
(skinny legend)

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:13.240
Oh yeah!

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:14.800
That's so cool.

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:16.080
*Mario pause sound effect*

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:17.080
How do they film that?

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:18.300
He starts off on the left,

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:19.151
then he's on the right,

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:20.240
and then he's on the left again.

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:23.160
And it's all one smooth camera shot the whole time.

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:25.460
The only real plate is the last one,

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:28.569
and the other two because he's mid-air, slash, like, away from camera

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:30.360
Those are probably gonna be plates.

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:31.820
I mean, that's how I would do it.

00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:32.840
Those first two guys,

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:34.200
you never see his feet.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:36.860
And, I think the thing that really signifies what's real and what's not, is–

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:37.870
Look at his hair throughout all of it.

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:40.280
So right there, you can see not a lot of detail on the hair,

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:41.820
you get the big strands without the tiny strands,

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:43.020
Same there.

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:44.800
You get kinda big strands without the tiny strands.

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:46.090
And now let's look at his hair here

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:48.850
Yeah, I can see that really fine hair detail on top of his head now.

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:50.630
He stabs the truck,

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:52.630
The cuts were already on the truck,

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:54.040
as were the swords,

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:56.075
and he just comes in and grabs the swords,

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:59.220
and they just paint him out for this few frames before he gets there.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:03.220
All right, so one of them is actually a bear–

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:04.180
I guess–
Whoa, whoa, wait–

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:04.800
What the–

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:06.120
(laughing)

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:07.140
Bear man??

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:08.840
He's a man bear!!

00:06:09.140 --> 00:06:10.540
Sometimes, he's full bear, too,

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:13.589
Which I don't understand why he'd ever become full bear when he–

00:06:13.589 --> 00:06:14.780
when man bear does this.

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:16.780
(ratatata)

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:19.760
*Mario pause sound effect*

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:21.400
Let's say you have transformation powers,

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:24.720
Do you ever have the forethought to maybe take your shirt off before you transform?

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:25.640
(laughing)

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:27.700
Like, or– is it always, like,

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:29.780
you're in such a grave situation that you're like, "no time."

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:30.400
(mimicking ripping)

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:31.600
And you shred that garment?

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:33.700
I think the reason for that, Sam, is just that

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:35.740
it always looks cooler to have clothes rip apart.

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:38.240
It looks way cooler to have a cloth-sim on your bear-sim.

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:39.220
(chuckle)

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:41.020
(roar)

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:42.120
(fighting noises)

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:43.940
(laughing)

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:45.320
He's got frikin' bear pecs!!

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:45.860
Dude!

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:47.800
What-? Did you see how that guy folded??

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:48.380
He was just like–

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:49.060
*snap*

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:50.080
(laughing)

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:51.660
His legs just like– pop up!

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:52.400
He frikin' folds.

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:54.100
It looks like when we made Marvel Rated-R.

00:06:54.129 --> 00:06:56.871
It's like that 'cause that's an element that they filmed either separately

00:06:56.871 --> 00:06:57.759
or had it in that shot

00:06:57.759 --> 00:06:59.660
but needed to re-time it and move it differently.

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:00.880
The Bear's like–

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:02.079
The Bear's... not bad.

00:07:02.379 --> 00:07:04.840
The bear in Dark Materials looks better in my opinion.

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:07.599
I think there's a lot of CG bears that look better than 
this bear.

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:10.460
But also those bears aren't transforming into a half man.

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:12.120
That– That transformation at the beginning though

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:15.120
really reminded me of that werewolf short from
 Love, Death, &amp; Robots

00:07:15.129 --> 00:07:18.240
We gotta talk about Love, Death, &amp; Robots 
at some point soon

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:20.224
because we have gotten so many comments about that

00:07:20.224 --> 00:07:21.060
and it's amazing.

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:22.960
So subscribe if you want to see that in the future.

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:30.700
All right, so we have Ninja,

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:31.660
we have Bear Man,

00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:32.759
uh- there's another one

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:34.320
I haven't introduced you to: Rock Man.

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:36.860
And then there's a girl, I don't know what her powers are.

00:07:36.862 --> 00:07:37.620
Is that rock man?

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:38.820
No, that's bad man.

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:39.980
Bad man. Gotcha.

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:42.080
Dude, Sloth from the Goonies.

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:44.300
I was thinking the same thing!

00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:46.620
This is like– This is practically like watching a Bollywood movie.

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:48.340
Yeah, this is very Bollywood.

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:51.680
(aaaahhhhh)

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:53.760
(AAAAHHHH)

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:55.880
*boof*

00:07:56.460 --> 00:07:58.100
The bear takes ONE punch.

00:07:58.100 --> 00:07:59.520
It's just barely–

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:01.120
The bear gets punched and the head is down.

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:06.380
There's a plane–

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:08.100
Throw my sword at it,

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:09.520
Giving them ropes.

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:10.800
The bear–

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:12.260
(laughing)

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:16.180
*Mario pause sound effect*

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:17.600
That's a very Bollywood shot right there.

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:19.370
Yeah, they're not TOO worried about physics.

00:08:19.370 --> 00:08:22.780
I would be so jazzed up if I was a teenager in Russia and this came out.

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:23.380
Yeah.

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:25.060
Yeah, this is what I think.

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:27.020
I think the effects are good enough.

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:28.040
Yeah, I agree.

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:30.400
They've got some rough edges,

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:31.600
but they work.

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:33.640
They're not really going for photo realism.

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:34.640
I think at the end of the day though

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:35.800
It's like, you got to get your movie made,

00:08:35.800 --> 00:08:37.527
and you got to tell your story, and,

00:08:37.527 --> 00:08:39.900
nothing in here is distracting from the story.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:41.478
Like, when I see the bear, I'm like,

00:08:41.478 --> 00:08:42.940
"Oh that wasn't really well done,

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:44.400
they really should have done better on that."

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:45.940
It's like– it's CG,

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:48.080
but I've already embraced that half this movie is CG anyways.

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:49.402
Like The Avengers, for example,

00:08:49.402 --> 00:08:50.809
That's basically an animated film.

00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:54.400
It's an animated film with, like, some human heads cut out and stuck onto the characters once in a while

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:57.640
And, like, this movie is entering that same territory where it's basically just an animated film.

00:08:57.640 --> 00:08:59.440
At least a good chunk of it, you know?

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:04.340
In order to really enjoy this movie at all, your suspension of disbelief has to accept that there's a–

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:07.100
Walking man bear with a gun turret.

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:10.940
So it's like, so much of this you've already kind of just tuned out as not real.

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:12.600
Yeah, you're like: "Oh the animation's weird."

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:15.320
It's like: "Well okay, what would a bear fighting a mech really look like?"

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:17.120
(laughing)

