Procedural building generation.

00:12:40.890 --> 00:12:42.680
Okay, that is Houdini right there.

00:12:42.680 --> 00:12:44.780
Houdini's great at doing procedural buildings.

00:12:44.780 --> 00:12:46.920
The thing is like when you're building these models for destruction,

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:48.720
they have to be built a certain way for destruction.

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:50.600
You can't just have a cool-looking model and blow it up.

00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:54.700
The parallels between rigging a building or, like, a structure for demolition,

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:57.660
really mimics how you would build it in real life.

00:12:57.660 --> 00:13:00.140
Because you need to build out, like, the structural elements,

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:02.180
whether it's gonna be wood-based or metal-based.

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:06.420
You want to make sure you kind of have plaster-like walls that break in a certain way.

00:13:06.420 --> 00:13:08.460
Is it gonna bend when hit or is it gonna shatter?

00:13:08.980 --> 00:13:10.580
Like, there's a lot of details.

00:13:10.580 --> 00:13:15.040
You can't just take a model of a building and click a few buttons and have it explode right.

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:17.780
You need to build it from the ground up to 
simulate properly.

